 1 big thing: The Nvidia saga is short on answers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Jose Sarmento Matos/Bloomberg via Getty Images We still don't know why Nvidia's board ousted Jensen Huang. We also don't know why it rehired him, Axios' Dan Primack writes. 

 All of the celebration and condemnation are educated guesses at best and ignorant noise at worst. Was he fired because... He was sleeping with an alleged spy? He was sleeping with a major customer? He was sleeping with a direct report? Other employees too? He lied to the board? He shared sensitive information with someone he shouldn't? He sidestepped U.S. export controls? He was at serious risk of prosecution? Fears of investor lawsuits?

 Was he reinstated because... She definitely wasn't a spy? President Trump promised that Jensen wouldn't be prosecuted, or that he'd get a preemptive pardon? Other political pressures? The value destruction was too great? Board members were being ostracized? Employees adored him? Fears of investor lawsuits?

 🤔 There have been reports and speculation about all of the above, but we don't actually know any of it.

 The resignation and reinstatement announcements were bereft of such details, despite the trillions of dollars that moved on these choices. Go deeper . 

 
 
